A||||6||Black88GT||Fiero leaves me stranded ....||||9||
Z||000000||Black88GT||10-20-2000||10:20 PM||||Well I thought I was done with all this mechanic crap.  I pretty much replaced everything when I had the 3.4 installed a month or two ago....<P>Belts have been chirping on startup but it stops after the first shift or if I let it warm up.  Driving down the road tonight home from work, they start squeeling big time, all the way through 4th.  "Odd", I think to myself. So I get to the red light, my voltage light comes on.  Not good.  Before the light turns green I see the coolant light come on, and the temp gauge shoot up to the red.  The light turned green I drove about 50 feet to an open parking spot and pulled in.  Shut her down and pop the decklid.  It was dark and I didn't have a flash light, but I suspected the belt obviously.  Sure enough, it's snapped (felt around then pulled it out).  <P>Here's my question though.  There was no coolant in the tank, it was all over the place around the tank (in front of the car of course).  It didn't look like any hoses had busted though.  All of the hoses are new, and I do mean all (except the ones that run along the car) and the radiator is new.  The water pump is also new.  The alternator is not though.  Could I have done any damage to either the radiator (don't think so but I thought I would ask anyway), water pump, or alternator?  Why did the belt snap all of the sudden?  I know in a way its my fault because I should have gotten it straightened out, but I thought since the belts were brand new they just needed to get adjusted or something. <P><BR>TIA  <P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||9
Z||000001||HELI FLYER||10-20-2000||10:42 PM||||Well it sounds like you put off doing a very important adjustment. When you put the belt in did you set the belt deflection to the proper tension? Did you use a high quailty belt? also a loose belt means that its slipping on the pulleys and burning the sides of the belt(V grove).<BR>The belt that failed on you plays such important part.I would check the service manual and find what is the proper adjustment for belt tension, Its late here and i cant remember <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Im sorry to here that you were let down.<P>------------------<BR>WILL TRADE WIFE FOR 88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Max The Chainsaw||10-20-2000||10:43 PM||||Damage to the radiator? I seriously doubt it. When the belt gets thrown off, the water pump quits turning and an engine will overheat VERY quickly. Since the pressure cap on a Fiero is not the highest point in the cooling system, it will blow out a LOT of coolant under this condition.<P>Since the belt broke, there are a couple things possoble:<P>1. A bad belt. (Could be, but if it was new I'd doubt it)<P>2. Pulley's out of alignment. (More likely cause the belt to be thrown off instead of broken.)<P>3. A bearing going out of something the belt drives, like the Water Pump or Alternator. I'd tend to suspect something along this line  from your description. A bearing that's siezing would cause the belt squeal you describe and if it siezed, it would break a belt in an instant.<P>Try spinning the alternator and water pump by hand and see if you hear any strange noise or feel any sticking when your turn it. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Seanh||10-20-2000||10:51 PM||||I doubt you could have damaged the alt., waterpump, or radiator. I just think the belt broke and the water stoped circulating and caused it to boil over and overheat. But the thing wrong with that is that you said there was no water in the tank. Did the line under the overflow tank maybe come loose or somthing? That is the only thing I could think of. I doubt the lines split if you just replaced them. Check that one out first if you havent already. Also, the squeling could be comming from the old alt bearing going out. Sounds like that could be the most likly problem next to the belt just being inproperly tenshioned.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System<p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 10-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||10-21-2000||12:39 PM||||Heli-flyer: Please send a (wife) pic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> J/K||||reg||10
Z||000005||Black88GT||10-21-2000||03:41 PM||||Max, thanks for the help.  The alternator is one of the very few things I didn't replace last month with the engine swap.  It is probably the original one which has very high miles on it so it sounds like that could be the problem, I am about 90% positive the belts were properly tensioned. <BR>I was getting this weird clacking sound, I just tightened the pulleys and it went away but you could still here it a little.  I wonder if that was the alternator bearing?<P>thanks again ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Seanh||10-21-2000||06:00 PM||||Its a bearing alright, most likely the alt, but it really could be any of the belt driven devices. If you can, take the belt off and try turning different devices by hand to see if you can isolate the problem.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8<BR>Killer Stereo System||||reg||1
